## Context
Nomination from the sen-causal-reading session (2026-10-15). The author does
not want harness skills to overload runtime names and prefers draconic names
for user-facing skills.

Observed in Claude Code 2.1.300 (binary strings): `dream` is a native
background task type (auto-dream memory consolidation, setting
`autoDreamEnabled`, task label "dream"). No `/dream` slash command string was
found, so the overload is a label and concept collision with the native
consolidation, not a command shadowing. Cause of user confusion: two
different consolidations, two different stores, one name.

Chosen name: `hoard` (author decision, 2026-10-15).
Historical context: `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-nomenclature.md` reserves
`hoard` for the memory library and its CLI verbs (`hoard assay`, `reckon`,
`seal`, `sift`, `shed`, `disown`, `weigh`), and states "the pass is /dream".
No code implements that library (no `hoard` file or package; memory v8
docs do not use the word). The author releases that reservation for the skill.

The author also chose `memory/HOARD.md` and `memory/hoards/`, with migration
of existing files and references. No transition alias, compatibility symlink,
dual-format operation or legacy entry point is retained.

On explicit `/hoard <project>` invocation, the skill automatically migrates
that project's old paths and references in its isolated project branch before
consolidation. `--dry-run` describes the migration without writing. If an old
path and its new counterpart both exist, stop with a visible conflict; never
overwrite either. Migration preserves file contents and provenance, updates
link targets and operational references, and is a no-op on an already migrated
project. Detection of old paths serves only this one-way migration; subsequent
consolidation uses the new paths exclusively.

## Exit criteria
- [x] Author decision recorded: `hoard`; historical library-name reservation
      released; migrate to `memory/HOARD.md` and `memory/hoards/`; no aliases.
- [x] Skill directory, frontmatter `name`, every reference (16 files in
      skills, rules, tests, Makefile, scripts, hooks, adapters, profiles;
      `lair` step 11, brood, roar) migrated to the new name and paths. Recount
      live references before implementation; the original 16-file estimate
      is not a fixed inventory. Historical record contents remain preserved.
- [x] `/hoard` automatically migrates an old-format project in its isolated
      branch before consolidation, including file moves and affected references.
      No cross-project sweep runs merely because the skill is invoked.
- [x] Migration is idempotent, preserves contents/provenance and fails without
      overwriting when old and new paths coexist; missing/unreadable inputs
      are reported rather than treated as empty. Dry-run makes no writes.
- [x] No `/dream` alias, compatibility symlink, old-format consolidation path
      or legacy runtime entry point remains after migration. Installed skill
      registrations and active project links resolve to the new names/paths.
- [x] Migration verification covers a fresh new-format project, an old-format
      project with linked reports, a repeated invocation, dry-run, and coexistence
      conflicts, including preservation of report contents and provenance.
- [x] `make skills-catalog` regenerated; `make check-skills-drift` and
      `make check` pass.

